1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,924
You can answer the first question now.
Git LFS keeps large files outside Git and

2
00:00:08,964 --> 00:00:13,554
leaves a small pointer file in their place,
but only for files added after tracking

3
00:00:13,594 --> 00:00:17,962
began. Here the blob is in a commit being
pushed, and deleting the file added a

4
00:00:18,002 --> 00:00:22,649
commit without removing one. The engineer's
next attempt is git lfs track, and a new

5
00:00:22,689 --> 00:00:25,104
commit. Still rejected,
for the same reason.

6
00:00:25,444 --> 00:00:30,157
The tool that does help is a history rewrite:
new commits that take the place of the

7
00:00:30,197 --> 00:00:34,231
old ones. And the same tool, with one
extra flag that promises to "catch

8
00:00:34,271 --> 00:00:38,928
everything", leaves a branch that's five
commits ahead of the server and one behind

9
00:00:38,968 --> 00:00:42,040
it, sharing not a single
commit with what the team has.

10
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:47,222
The second question has an answer that
surprises people who think in Git terms: on

11
00:00:47,262 --> 00:00:50,735
the hosting side, storage doesn't
shrink when history does.

12
00:00:50,775 --> 00:00:54,665
Hold on to that second question.
Its answer is one row of a table.

13
00:00:56,505 --> 00:01:00,415
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:00,455 --> 00:01:03,848
This is the last video on
LFS, and it has four parts.

15
00:01:03,888 --> 00:01:07,410
git lfs migrate, for files
that are already in history.

16
00:01:07,450 --> 00:01:11,555
Three errors that you'll meet,
each recognised from its message.

17
00:01:11,595 --> 00:01:16,542
GitHub's file limits and how it bills
for LFS, stated from its documentation.

18
00:01:16,582 --> 00:01:21,593
And the judgement call: when LFS is the
wrong tool and what the textbook names

19
00:01:21,633 --> 00:01:22,111
instead.

20
00:01:22,451 --> 00:01:26,923
Everything about rewriting history from
the rebase module applies here: new commit

21
00:01:26,963 --> 00:01:30,554
IDs, and the difference between
published and unpublished commits.

22
00:01:30,594 --> 00:01:33,910
Two of the replays are volatile
and will be marked on screen.

23
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:41,333
say what git lfs migrate import does to
commit IDs, the working tree and the size of

24
00:01:41,373 --> 00:01:41,668
.git;

25
00:01:42,008 --> 00:01:45,509
plan a migration of a shared
repository as a history rewrite;

26
00:01:45,849 --> 00:01:50,042
diagnose three LFS errors from their messages:
a missing object, a file that should

27
00:01:50,082 --> 00:01:52,337
have been a pointer, a
file tracked too late;

28
00:01:52,677 --> 00:01:59,223
state GitHub's file limits and the LFS
billing model as the cited section gives

29
00:01:59,263 --> 00:01:59,640
them;

30
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:07,661
decide when LFS is the wrong tool and
name the alternative the section gives.

31
00:02:09,501 --> 00:02:10,683
git lfs migrate has three modes.

32
00:02:11,023 --> 00:02:15,231
info reads history and reports
sizes by file extension.

33
00:02:15,271 --> 00:02:17,317
It changes nothing. 🟢 SAFE.

34
00:02:17,657 --> 00:02:22,089
import is a history rewrite. 🟡 CAUTION for
the default scope: it replaces matching

35
00:02:22,129 --> 00:02:26,615
blobs by pointers in every commit in scope,
adds the .gitattributes line, and gives

36
00:02:26,655 --> 00:02:29,833
every rewritten commit and all
of its descendants a new ID.

37
00:02:30,173 --> 00:02:34,009
export is the reverse rewrite: pointers
become ordinary blobs again in the commits

38
00:02:34,049 --> 00:02:34,435
in scope.

39
00:02:34,775 --> 00:02:39,647
Scope is the dangerous option. By default
all three modes look only at the current

40
00:02:39,687 --> 00:02:44,440
branch, and only at commits that are on
no remote. That default is the safe one:

41
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:49,232
nothing that anyone else has is rewritten.
Name further branches as arguments to

42
00:02:49,272 --> 00:02:51,090
include their unpushed commits.

43
00:02:51,430 --> 00:02:56,546
--everything means every commit reachable
from every local and remote ref, including

44
00:02:56,586 --> 00:02:58,941
commits that are already on the remote.

45
00:02:59,281 --> 00:03:03,357
🔴 DANGEROUS when followed by a forced
push: it rewrites published history.

46
00:03:03,697 --> 00:03:08,639
Back to the default scope. Two things about
the state import leaves surprise people.

47
00:03:08,679 --> 00:03:13,029
First, the working tree file is a
pointer afterwards, even with the filter

48
00:03:13,069 --> 00:03:17,952
installed. The command's own help says this
is normal and names the remedy, git lfs

49
00:03:17,992 --> 00:03:21,036
checkout. Second, the
repository didn't get smaller.

50
00:03:21,376 --> 00:03:26,668
The old commits are off the branch and
still in the reflog, Git's local journal of

51
00:03:26,708 --> 00:03:32,130
where a branch has been. So the old blobs
are still in the object database until the

52
00:03:32,170 --> 00:03:35,512
reflog entries expire and
a garbage collection runs.

53
00:03:35,552 --> 00:03:40,909
And the bytes haven't left your disk either:
they are in .git/lfs/objects now, once

54
00:03:40,949 --> 00:03:41,689
per version.

55
00:03:42,029 --> 00:03:47,022
When the large files are already on the
remote, a rewrite of published history is

56
00:03:47,062 --> 00:03:50,999
what you need, and it's an operation
with a plan, not a command.

57
00:03:51,039 --> 00:03:56,156
Everyone stops pushing. One person rewrites
and pushes with --force-with-lease, the

58
00:03:56,196 --> 00:04:00,941
forced push that first checks what the
server has. Everyone else re-clones or

59
00:04:00,981 --> 00:04:04,420
resets. Old clones that push
again bring the blobs back.

60
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:09,964
There's also git lfs migrate import
--no-rewrite, which converts files in one new

61
00:04:10,004 --> 00:04:15,142
commit without touching earlier ones. It
has the limit you would expect: the old

62
00:04:15,182 --> 00:04:20,385
blobs stay in history, so it doesn't help
with a push that's rejected for a large

63
00:04:20,425 --> 00:04:20,709
file.

64
00:04:21,049 --> 00:04:26,767
Three errors, one family. In each, a path
that .gitattributes assigns to LFS, the

65
00:04:26,807 --> 00:04:30,820
blob in Git, and the object
in the LFS store don't agree.

66
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:35,173
The file was committed before the pattern
was tracked: the blob is the raw file.

67
00:04:35,513 --> 00:04:39,609
A commit was made on a machine without the
filter: the blob is the raw file again,

68
00:04:39,649 --> 00:04:41,021
reached from the other side.

69
00:04:41,361 --> 00:04:45,471
The pointer was pushed and the object
wasn't: the blob is a correct pointer to

70
00:04:45,511 --> 00:04:49,939
content that the remote doesn't have. The
textbook points out the family resemblance

71
00:04:49,979 --> 00:04:54,194
to the unpushed submodule commit: a
reference was published without the thing it

72
00:04:54,234 --> 00:04:54,726
refers to.

73
00:04:55,066 --> 00:04:59,467
GitHub. Limits and billing are platform
behavior. Hold that for the on-screen

74
00:04:59,507 --> 00:05:03,619
segment, where every number is read
from the textbook's quotation of the

75
00:05:03,659 --> 00:05:04,426
documentation.

76
00:05:04,766 --> 00:05:10,021
When not to use LFS. LFS fits a modest
number of mid-sized binary files that change

77
00:05:10,061 --> 00:05:14,997
rarely and must be versioned together
with the code: reference checkpoints for

78
00:05:15,037 --> 00:05:20,165
tests, small fixtures, images and design
assets. The table of section 22.13 lists

79
00:05:20,205 --> 00:05:25,141
five situations in which it's the wrong
tool. You'll read it at the end of the

80
00:05:25,181 --> 00:05:26,034
demonstration.

81
00:05:27,874 --> 00:05:29,890
Use the coat check for the three errors.

82
00:05:30,230 --> 00:05:34,736
Tracked too late, or committed without the
filter: somebody walked past the ticket

83
00:05:34,776 --> 00:05:39,228
desk and hung the coat itself on the ticket
rail. The rail was built for tickets.

84
00:05:39,268 --> 00:05:42,721
Everyone who comes later finds a
coat where a ticket should be.

85
00:05:43,061 --> 00:05:47,672
Pointer pushed, object not: a ticket was
sent to the other building, and the coat it

86
00:05:47,712 --> 00:05:52,212
names never left yours. Whoever presents
the ticket there's told that no such coat

87
00:05:52,252 --> 00:05:52,599
exists.

88
00:05:52,939 --> 00:05:57,788
For migrate import, picture reprinting
the history books so that every page that

89
00:05:57,828 --> 00:06:02,616
used to have a coat stapled to it now has
a ticket, with the coats moved to the

90
00:06:02,656 --> 00:06:07,749
cloakroom. Reprinted pages have new page
numbers, and so does every page after them.

91
00:06:07,789 --> 00:06:11,905
The old print run is still in your
storeroom until you clear it out.

92
00:06:11,945 --> 00:06:16,916
The analogy breaks on purpose at the hosting
side: clearing your storeroom doesn't

93
00:06:16,956 --> 00:06:18,566
empty the host's cloakroom.

94
00:06:18,906 --> 00:06:22,804
Try it now, on paper, thirty
seconds. Draw five commits in a row.

95
00:06:22,844 --> 00:06:27,530
The first is already on the remote. The
second and third each add a model, the

96
00:06:27,570 --> 00:06:30,681
fourth doesn't touch it,
and the fifth adds another.

97
00:06:30,721 --> 00:06:33,892
Circle every commit whose
ID you predict will change.

98
00:06:40,232 --> 00:06:44,399
Check your circles. The commit that
was already on the remote kept its ID.

99
00:06:44,439 --> 00:06:48,663
The four above it are new commits with
the old messages, authors and dates.

100
00:06:48,703 --> 00:06:53,097
Look at the fourth row: that commit didn't
touch the model, and its ID changed

101
00:06:53,137 --> 00:06:56,678
anyway, because a commit ID is a
hash that includes the parent.

102
00:06:56,718 --> 00:06:59,463
If you left that one out,
you're in good company.

103
00:07:10,204 --> 00:07:15,348
Note the scope it chose by itself. The
line "Examining commits" ends at four of

104
00:07:15,388 --> 00:07:20,532
four. main is four commits ahead of
origin/main, so four commits were examined.

105
00:07:20,572 --> 00:07:23,026
3.8 megabytes of .onnx in three files.

106
00:07:23,366 --> 00:07:27,745
You predicted this one on paper: how
many of the five commit IDs change?

107
00:07:28,085 --> 00:07:32,918
Four. c66100a, which was already
on the remote, kept its ID.

108
00:07:33,258 --> 00:07:39,006
The rewritten first model commit, b1b921b,
adds .gitattributes and a three-line file

109
00:07:39,046 --> 00:07:44,725
where the model was. The rest of the snippet
shows three pointer blobs of 132 bytes

110
00:07:44,765 --> 00:07:47,688
where the branch reached
three large blobs.

111
00:07:48,028 --> 00:07:53,717
Now the first surprise. A quick quiz:
after the import, is the model file in the

112
00:07:53,757 --> 00:07:57,870
working tree its full size, or
132 bytes? Say it out loud.

113
00:08:02,710 --> 00:08:07,993
132 bytes, a minus in the listing. After
git lfs checkout, the real 1,300,000 bytes.

114
00:08:08,033 --> 00:08:11,478
The second surprise: the
repository didn't get smaller.

115
00:08:11,818 --> 00:08:17,510
Before the expiry, the reflog still names
8b13104, the old tip, and three blobs of

116
00:08:17,550 --> 00:08:23,033
seven-digit size are still in the object
database. After it, they are gone from

117
00:08:23,073 --> 00:08:23,871
Git's store.

118
00:08:24,211 --> 00:08:27,559
A flag that promises to catch
everything looks like the answer.

119
00:08:27,899 --> 00:08:33,400
Read the graph from the bottom: the root
commit is now e6c7da2, a rewritten copy of

120
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:34,468
the pushed root.

121
00:08:34,808 --> 00:08:38,634
The local branches no longer
share a commit with origin/main.

122
00:08:38,674 --> 00:08:43,704
That's the root-cause box on screen. Not
pushed yet, so the fix is in the branch

123
00:08:43,744 --> 00:08:46,112
reflogs, and the lab walks through it.

124
00:08:59,452 --> 00:09:03,018
New commit IDs again, above
the same unchanged c66100a.

125
00:09:03,058 --> 00:09:08,002
Export doesn't delete the tracking line.
It appends a line that switches the

126
00:09:08,042 --> 00:09:10,034
attributes off for the pattern.

127
00:09:16,174 --> 00:09:19,877
Predict what git status says
about a file nobody edited.

128
00:09:19,917 --> 00:09:21,681
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

129
00:09:26,521 --> 00:09:31,320
Three symptoms of one cause. git lfs
ls-files doesn't list the recording.

130
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:36,757
The blob in HEAD is 48,000 bytes, the raw
file. And git status reports the file as

131
00:09:36,797 --> 00:09:41,928
modified: the attributes now say "clean
this path", the clean filter turns the

132
00:09:41,968 --> 00:09:47,365
working tree file into a pointer, and the
pointer differs from the raw blob in the

133
00:09:47,405 --> 00:09:52,735
index. git lfs fsck --pointers names the
problem: "should have been a pointer but

134
00:09:52,775 --> 00:09:53,331
was not".

135
00:09:53,671 --> 00:09:58,870
The forward fix. 🟡 CAUTION: git add
--renormalize runs the path through the filter

136
00:09:58,910 --> 00:10:00,659
again and stages the result.

137
00:10:00,999 --> 00:10:03,428
"Bin 48000 -> 130 bytes."

138
00:10:03,768 --> 00:10:08,535
The raw blob is still in the earlier
commit. For a 48 kilobyte file that's

139
00:10:08,575 --> 00:10:13,862
acceptable. For a file that a host would
reject, use git lfs migrate import on the

140
00:10:13,902 --> 00:10:15,486
unpushed commits instead.

141
00:10:15,826 --> 00:10:21,596
No filter, and the attribute line is there.
The full 1,250,000 bytes went into Git

142
00:10:21,636 --> 00:10:24,642
as a blob. Asha, who
has the filter, pulls.

143
00:10:24,982 --> 00:10:27,670
"Bin 132 -> 1250000 bytes" in the
pull's summary is the first sign.

144
00:10:28,010 --> 00:10:33,236
The same diagnosis as before, and the file
is modified the moment it's checked out.

145
00:10:33,276 --> 00:10:38,185
The same fix, and the same limit: the
large blob stays in the history that was

146
00:10:38,225 --> 00:10:38,629
pushed.

147
00:10:38,969 --> 00:10:44,048
Prevention is a server-side or CI check,
because the failing machine is by

148
00:10:44,088 --> 00:10:49,374
definition one where the local setup is
missing: a job that runs git lfs fsck

149
00:10:49,414 --> 00:10:52,625
--pointers and fails on
a non-zero exit status.

150
00:10:55,465 --> 00:11:00,257
Here the hook was skipped with --no-verify.
The pointer is in the remote's Git

151
00:11:00,297 --> 00:11:03,974
history. The object exists only
in the author's local store.

152
00:11:04,014 --> 00:11:07,133
A teammate with a correctly
installed client pulls.

153
00:11:07,473 --> 00:11:10,316
"remote missing object", exit status
2, and a minus in the listing.

154
00:11:10,356 --> 00:11:12,166
The same failure through
the smudge filter:

155
00:11:12,506 --> 00:11:21,543
"Smudge error". Because the filter is
required, a failing smudge fails the checkout:

156
00:11:21,583 --> 00:11:25,757
the file is absent, not silently wrong.

157
00:11:26,097 --> 00:11:31,878
The pointer names f8719e1. The remote's
store has one file, and it isn't that one.

158
00:11:32,218 --> 00:11:37,587
Whoever still has the object uploads it.
Predict whether a plain git lfs push origin

159
00:11:37,627 --> 00:11:39,132
main does it. I'll wait.

160
00:11:43,972 --> 00:11:48,893
No, and almost everyone expects a yes. The
client skips objects of commits that the

161
00:11:48,933 --> 00:11:51,283
remote-tracking branch already contains.

162
00:11:51,623 --> 00:11:56,711
git lfs push --all origin main sends
every object the branch references.

163
00:11:56,751 --> 00:12:02,694
If nobody has the object any more, the
pointer is permanently dangling, and the only

164
00:12:02,734 --> 00:12:06,041
repair is a new commit
with a file that exists.

165
00:12:06,381 --> 00:12:09,491
Without LFS, the section
quotes four limits.

166
00:12:09,831 --> 00:12:13,782
A warning from Git for a file
larger than 50 mebibytes.

167
00:12:13,822 --> 00:12:19,806
"GitHub blocks files larger than 100 MiB".
A file added through the browser "can be

168
00:12:19,846 --> 00:12:25,757
no larger than 25 MiB". And repositories
should "remain small, ideally less than 1

169
00:12:25,797 --> 00:12:31,781
GB, and less than 5 GB is strongly recommended".
It adds a cap of 2 gigabytes for a

170
00:12:31,821 --> 00:12:37,805
single push. The block applies to any commit
in the push that contains such a blob.

171
00:12:38,145 --> 00:12:43,775
With LFS, the maximum size of one LFS
file depends on the plan: 2 gigabytes on

172
00:12:43,815 --> 00:12:49,153
GitHub Free and Pro, 4 gigabytes on
Team, 5 gigabytes on Enterprise Cloud.

173
00:12:52,493 --> 00:12:56,904
The last row is the answer to the storage-bill
question, the one you were holding on

174
00:12:56,944 --> 00:12:57,063
to.

175
00:13:02,403 --> 00:13:07,560
The textbook notes that the versions,
licences and stewardship of these tools

176
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:12,960
changed during 2025 and 2026, and that
the AI and ML workflows chapter gives the

177
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,187
current facts with their sources.

178
00:13:17,027 --> 00:13:18,675
Five mistakes to watch for.

179
00:13:19,015 --> 00:13:22,723
git lfs track and a new commit
to fix a rejected push.

180
00:13:22,763 --> 00:13:28,413
Root cause: the large blob is still in an
earlier commit of the push; only migrate

181
00:13:28,453 --> 00:13:30,911
import on those commits replaces it.

182
00:13:31,251 --> 00:13:34,615
migrate import --everything
to "catch the other branch".

183
00:13:34,655 --> 00:13:39,355
Root cause: it puts published commits in
scope, and the first rewritten commit

184
00:13:39,395 --> 00:13:44,399
changes the ID of every descendant, so local
history no longer shares a commit with

185
00:13:44,439 --> 00:13:45,068
the remote.

186
00:13:45,408 --> 00:13:50,260
Expecting .git to shrink right after a
migration. Root cause: the old commits are

187
00:13:50,300 --> 00:13:54,911
still in the reflog and their blobs in
the object database until expiry and a

188
00:13:54,951 --> 00:13:55,575
collection.

189
00:13:55,915 --> 00:14:01,473
git lfs push origin main to
repair a missing object.

190
00:14:01,513 --> 00:14:09,976
Root cause: the client skips objects of
commits that the remote-tracking branch

191
00:14:10,016 --> 00:14:16,220
already has; --all sends
everything the branch references.

192
00:14:16,560 --> 00:14:21,844
Deleting files from LFS to reduce the GitHub
bill. Root cause: according to GitHub's

193
00:14:21,884 --> 00:14:27,105
documentation the objects still exist on
the remote storage and keep counting until

194
00:14:27,145 --> 00:14:31,098
the repository is deleted and
recreated or Support purges them.

195
00:14:32,938 --> 00:14:37,519
Now, out of the lab. A speech team
discovers that eleven model versions were

196
00:14:37,559 --> 00:14:42,078
committed as ordinary files over a year,
and all of them are on the server.

197
00:14:42,118 --> 00:14:44,875
A new hire's first clone
takes twenty minutes.

198
00:14:45,215 --> 00:14:48,802
The lead treats it as a planned
rewrite of published history.

199
00:14:48,842 --> 00:14:53,678
She runs migrate info with the scope she
intends to use and reads the commit count

200
00:14:53,718 --> 00:14:58,256
aloud in the planning meeting. She stores
a verified bundle of the old state.

201
00:14:58,296 --> 00:15:00,694
On the agreed day everyone stops pushing.

202
00:15:01,034 --> 00:15:06,634
She runs the import, checks the result in
a fresh clone, and pushes with a lease.

203
00:15:06,674 --> 00:15:12,205
Everyone else re-clones, because an old
clone that pushes again brings the blobs

204
00:15:12,245 --> 00:15:17,149
back. A CI job that runs git lfs fsck
--pointers goes in the same week.

205
00:15:17,489 --> 00:15:22,144
Then the team reads the table of section
22.13 and makes a second decision.

206
00:15:22,184 --> 00:15:27,090
The reference checkpoints for tests stay
in LFS: a handful of files that change

207
00:15:27,130 --> 00:15:32,350
twice a year. The weights produced by every
training run move out of Git altogether,

208
00:15:32,390 --> 00:15:37,108
to a store the team controls, with the
Git commit recorded beside each model

209
00:15:37,148 --> 00:15:42,179
version. The bill had been growing because
every run stored a whole new file that

210
00:15:42,219 --> 00:15:43,682
nothing would ever free.

211
00:15:45,522 --> 00:15:51,573
Your turn. Do Lab 15.4, "Migrate an
already-committed large file", in

212
00:15:51,613 --> 00:15:55,281
